                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose: learners, objective and the minute budget`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The brief lands: a flagship 20-minute leadership module for <strong>busy, mobile-first senior managers</strong>, benchmarked in six weeks against two rivals. Before designing anything, decide how adults actually learn in short formats — relevance, active processing, immediate application. Commit to <strong>one sharp learning objective</strong> you can prove in 20 minutes, state the <em>design principle</em> the module will obey, and decide how it opens. Remember the Lab’s data: a busy executive is asking “why should I care in the next 30 seconds?” — <em>hook them before you teach them</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Plan: select the format and sequence the blocks`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Storyboard the module. Allocate minutes across <strong>passive content</strong>, <strong>experiential</strong>, <strong>retrieval / interaction</strong> and <strong>hook &amp; transitions</strong> — the four must sum to exactly <strong>18</strong>, leaving a 2-minute reserve for the assessment you design next round. The Lab’s engagement-decay data is the constraint: passive content past <em>~6–7 minutes</em> stops landing and learners drift to email. The comprehensive, content-rich design overruns and crowds out application; the lean experiential design risks under-teaching. Decide where experiential beats content — and where a short content scaffold is non-negotiable so the experience pays off.`,
