                    coach_default:          `Design choice logged.`,
                    coach_r1_broad:         `<strong>“Cover it comprehensively” is the trap before you have written a line.</strong> Twenty minutes cannot survey a field. A broad objective means every block is thin and nothing is provable — and the client asked for <em>demonstrated</em> learning. Pick one sharp, applicable skill you can prove in 20 minutes.`,
                    coach_r1_awareness:     `<strong>“Raise awareness” is a smile-sheet objective.</strong> It feels safe, but it gives you nothing to measure and nothing to retrieve — so retention and the benchmark suffer. A single applicable skill is what a busy executive can use on Monday.`,
                    coach_r1_coverage:      `<strong>“The more we explain, the more they learn” is exactly the belief the Lab’s data refutes.</strong> Coverage is not learning in a short format — past minute seven, more explainer means <em>less</em> retained. Make active processing, not volume, your principle.`,
                    coach_r1_polish:        `<strong>Production values help, but they do not teach.</strong> A beautiful 12-minute explainer still loses senior managers at minute seven. Polish buys a little goodwill; it cannot substitute for relevance, retrieval and application.`,
                    coach_r1_theory:        `<strong>Your first 90 seconds define the concept in the abstract — and you have already lost them.</strong> A busy executive is asking “why should I care in the next 30 seconds?” Adults engage when it is relevant and problem-centred <em>first</em>. Hook them before you teach them.`,
                    coach_r1_agenda:        `<strong>A learning-objectives slide is honest, but it is not a hook.</strong> Telling people the running order does not make them care. Open with a dilemma from their own week and the agenda takes care of itself.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> One provable objective, an experiential-first principle, and an opening that earns attention with relevance before teaching. That is exactly how a 20-minute module holds busy executives — and it sets up every later choice to pay off.`,
                    coach_r2_stuffed:       `<strong>Your storyboard is mostly explainer — and that is the content-stuffing trap.</strong> The Lab’s data says engagement falls off a cliff at minute seven; by minute ten half your senior managers are answering email. You taught a lot and they retained little. What would you cut so the concept actually lands?`,
                    coach_r2_unscaffolded:  `<strong>You went all-in on one long experience with no content scaffold.</strong> Half the cohort does not have the concept to make sense of it — experts love it, novices flounder, and engagement splits. Where does a 90-second content block make the experiential time pay off?`,
                    coach_r2_contentfirst:  `<strong>Content first, experience last “if time allows” means experience never happens.</strong> The application gets squeezed out and you have front-loaded the passive block straight into the decay zone. Weave relevance and application throughout instead.`,
                    coach_r3_selfrating:    `<strong>“Rate your confidence 1–5” tells you they feel good, not that they learned.</strong> The client asked for <em>demonstrated</em> knowledge retention — a confidence score is a smile sheet. A lightweight retrieval or scenario proves learning without eating five minutes.`,
                    coach_r3_longquiz:      `<strong>A 6-minute quiz proves retention — and starves the module.</strong> That is nearly a third of your budget on testing, crowding out the experiential blocks that earn engagement. Strong evidence, but at a price the 20 minutes cannot afford. A short applied scenario gets you most of the proof for a fraction of the minutes.`,
                    coach_r3_noassess:      `<strong>No minutes on assessment means no evidence of learning.</strong> The client benchmarks you on demonstrated retention. Even a two-minute applied scenario gives you a number you can defend.`,
                    coach_r3_uniform:       `<strong>Uniform 5-minute segments back to back ignore the decay curve.</strong> You lose people <em>inside</em> each segment. The lever is interaction cadence — how often the learner <em>does</em> something rather than watches.`,
                    coach_r3_onelong:       `<strong>One long uninterrupted segment is the surest way to lose mobile learners.</strong> Fewer interruptions sounds tidy, but attention decays within the segment and there is no beat to pull them back. Break it up and make them act.`,
                    coach_r3_cutexp:        `<strong>Bolting a long compliance slide on and cutting the experience is the wrong trade.</strong> Accessibility should be built into the design, not bought with the experiential minutes that do the teaching. You met the letter of the requirement and weakened the learning.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring the minute-11 drop-off and the accessibility ask is a benchmark risk.</strong> The drop-off is exactly where completion bleeds, and an inaccessible module fails learners and the client’s requirement. A re-engagement beat and built-in captions cost no minutes.`,
                    coach_r3_strong:        `<strong>That is designing for the curve.</strong> An applied scenario that proves and strengthens retention, short segments with frequent interaction, and a re-engagement beat right at the minute-11 cliff with accessibility built in. Completion and retention both move — and you spent the minutes where they pay.`,
                    coach_r4_metrics:       `<strong>Leading with the benchmark metrics is what the client came to hear.</strong> Completion, retention and satisfaction — and which design choice moved each — is a defence two rivals will struggle to match. You are speaking their language and the numbers back you.`,
                    coach_r4_effort:        `<strong>Leading with “how much we packed in” invites the obvious question.</strong> The client does not buy effort; they buy outcomes. Coverage as a defence concedes that you designed for volume, not for how the cohort actually learns.`,
                    coach_r4_smiles:        `<strong>Leading with the satisfaction score alone is the smile-sheet defence.</strong> A high rating without demonstrated retention tells the client people enjoyed it, not that it worked — and the rivals will show the learning numbers you did not.`,
                    coach_r4_coverage:      `<strong>“Coverage proves rigour” is the rationale the Lab’s data disproves.</strong> A serious short module proves it taught one thing, not that it mentioned ten. Mapping blocks to learning principles is the rigorous story; volume is not.`,
                    coach_r4_instinct:      `<strong>Instinct is not a rationale a client benchmarks against.</strong> “We trusted our experience” cannot be compared to a rival’s evidence. Map each block to a principle so the design can be defended on its merits.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>That owns the trade-off honestly.</strong> Naming what you cut to protect attention and evidence — and why it serves the one objective — is exactly what a client trusts. You did not claim a free lunch or blame the learners; you designed deliberately, and the four metrics show it.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>“There was no trade-off” is the claim a benchmark exposes.</strong> Twenty minutes is zero-sum; every minute on content is a minute not on application. Claiming you had it all ways tells the client you have not understood the constraint.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming busy executives for not engaging is the designer abdicating the job.</strong> Holding distractible, mobile-first learners is precisely what good short-format design does. The disengagement was a design outcome, not a learner failing.`,

