                    insight_won:            `Benchmark won: you designed for how adults actually learn in a short format — a sharp objective, a relevance hook, lean content kept inside the decay zone, real experiential time with retrieval, an applied scenario that proves learning, and safeguards at the minute-11 cliff. Engagement, retention, completion and satisfaction all clear the rivals, and the module fits in 20 minutes. The NOK 9M account is defended.`,
                    insight_stuffed:        `Content-stuffing trap: you packed the 20 minutes with passive explainer past the ~6–7 minute decay onset, so engagement fell off the cliff and learners drifted to email. You taught a lot and they retained little — coverage is not learning in a short format, and completion and engagement are capped no matter how the later rounds played.`,
                    insight_unscaffolded:   `Cohort split: one long experiential block with no content scaffold left novices without the concept to make sense of it. Experts engaged, novices floundered, and retention is capped — a short content beat would have made the experiential minutes pay off for everyone.`,
                    insight_weakassess:     `No proof of learning: a confidence self-rating tells you learners felt good, not that they learned. The client asked for demonstrated retention and benchmarked you on it — without retrieval or an applied scenario, retention is unproven and capped.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed result: parts of the design worked, but the objective, the minute budget, the assessment and the attention safeguards did not fully reinforce each other — enough gaps for at least one benchmark metric to fall short of the rivals.`,
                    insight_theory:         `You opened in the abstract: defining the concept before establishing relevance lost busy, mobile-first executives in the first 90 seconds, and engagement never fully recovered. Andragogy is blunt — relevance and a problem-centred hook come first.`,
